From 66934fccab0d1ed6bcf81a7f7d836dbda759cd41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 01:56:35 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 20/30] Make the xsimd swizzle templates work on big-endian
 targets

The generic premultiply, unpremultiply and CMYK templates in
SwizzleGeneric.h treated every pixel as a little-endian 32-bit lane: the
alpha was taken from the top byte, the R/B channels were the low byte of
each 16-bit lane and the G/A channels the high byte. On a big-endian
target a B8G8R8A8 pixel is 0xBBGGRRAA, so the alpha is the low byte and
the two 16-bit halves swap roles, and every xsimd instantiation produced
wrong pixels there (the scalar fallbacks already handle both byte orders).

Add SplitEvenOdd_SIMD and CombineEvenOdd_SIMD, which isolate the channels
at even (R/B, C/Y) and odd (G/A, M/K) byte positions into the low byte of
a 16-bit lane on either endianness, and endian-selected constants for the
alpha and green masks. The byte-level operations (R/B swap, RGB24 expand)
were already endian-neutral. The VSX helpers get the same treatment for
their partial loads, unpremultiply reverse step and RGB24 alpha, and the
VSX swizzle path is now built on big-endian PowerPC as well.
